    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d293">

                <pb facs="0479" n="165" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg165"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/326</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-08-11T02:00:00+00:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 11, 1914, 2 a.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 8 a.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram—Extract]</p>
                <p>323. Sir Edward Grey has conferred with me about suggestions that he says you
                    have made through the British Embassy at Washington <pb facs="0480" n="166"
                        xml:id="pg166"/> concerning the possible neutralization of the Pacific
                    Ocean. He fears that so sweeping an arrangement could hardly be made. But
                    suggestion from the United States to England and Germany to agree that <hi
                        rendition="#italic">status quo</hi> in China be maintained by each of them
                    would be a great advantage if agreed to. Grey must consult British Cabinet
                    before committing himself but there is little doubt in my mind about the
                    Cabinet’s acceptance. Please consider whether our Government will make such a
                    suggestion and inform me. . . .</p>
                <p> Grey’s concern is to prevent fighting in China or in Chinese waters for fear of
                    causing disturbance in China.</p>
                <closer>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
